<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kyrgyzstan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kyrgyz Republic, Kirghizia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kyrgyzstan, officially the Kyrgyz Republic and formerly Kirghizia, is a landlocked country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, lying in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a> and Pamir mountain ranges. Its lands were part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>, and it was first established as the Yenisei Kyrgyz Khaganate. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> conquered it in the 13th century, and later the Dzungar Khanate invaded; Kyrgyz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a> then became part of the Kokand Khanate. In 1876 Kyrgyzstan became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, and in 1936 the Kirghiz Soviet Socialist Republic was formed as a constituent republic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/askar-akayev">Askar Akayev</a> was elected president in 1990, and Kyrgyzstan declared independence on 31 August 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan.jpg" alt="Kyrgyzstan" width="330" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shakiev N.T., Primov U.B. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kyrgyzstan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Kyrgyz state reached its widest extent after defeating the Uyghur Khaganate in 840 CE. From the 10th century the Kyrgyz migrated as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a> range and kept their dominance there for about 200 years. A storytelling tradition, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-manas">Epic of Manas</a>, tells of a warrior who unified the scattered tribes into one nation in the 9th century; the trilogy is on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Intangible Cultural Heritage List and expresses the memory of the nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyz-people">Kyrgyz people</a>. One proposed etymology of the name is 'forty tribes', which may refer to the forty clans of Manas.</p><p>In the late 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a> ceded much of the Issyk-Kul Region to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> through the Treaty of Tarbagatai, and the territory, called Kirghizia in Russian, was formally incorporated in 1876. The Russian takeover met numerous revolts, and many Kyrgyz moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamir Mountains</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Soviet power was established in 1919 with the Kara-Kyrgyz Autonomous Oblast, a name Russians used until the mid-1920s to distinguish the Kyrgyz from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a>, who were also called Kirghiz. The Kirghiz Soviet Socialist Republic was established on 5 December 1936.</p><p>In October 1991 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/askar-akayev">Askar Akayev</a> ran unopposed and was elected president of the new republic with 95 per cent of the vote. On 8 December the leaders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> signed the Belavezha Accords, declaring that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> would cease to exist. In 2005 parliamentary elections, which the opposition and international observers criticised for irregularities, led to protests known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulip-revolution">Tulip Revolution</a>, and Akayev fled the country and resigned in April.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kyrgyzstan lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a> and Pamir ranges, and the Tian Shan covers over 80 per cent of the country. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishkek">Bishkek</a>, on the northern border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, is the capital and largest city. The population was estimated at 7,404,300 in January 2026, with Kyrgyz 77.8 per cent and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> 14.3 per cent. Kyrgyz is the state language and Russian an official one. Herding families still move to high summer pastures, the jailoo. Shyrdak and ala-kiyiz felt carpets were inscribed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s list in need of urgent safeguarding in 2012.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kyrgyzstan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…1991 and the United Nations on 2 March 1992, and belongs to the OSCE, the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation, the Organisation of Turkic States and the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation; it joined GUAM in 1999 and left in 2005, and founded the Central Asian Union with Kazakhstan and <b>Kyrgyzstan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…as one of the world’s oldest religions, and since the dissolution of the Soviet Union it has spread among intellectuals in Kazakhstan, <b>Kyrgyzstan</b>, Tatarstan and Bashkortostan and is undergoing an organised revival in Siberia; Nursultan Nazarbayev and Askar Akayev called it the “natural” religion of the Turkic peoples.</blockquote><a href="/religion/tengrism">Tengrism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Orkhon inscriptions, which stand in the Orkhon valley of Mongolia, whose cultural landscape is a World Heritage Site, are revered as national monuments in Turkey, Kazakhstan, <b>Kyrgyzstan</b> and other Turkic countries, and their words are read in schools.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-turkic">Old Turkic · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The names of modern assemblies, Mongolia’s State Great Khural and the kurultais of Kazakhstan, <b>Kyrgyzstan</b> and Bashkortostan, recall the old assembly, and Mongolia’s Naadam festival keeps the wrestling, horse racing and archery that accompanied it.</blockquote><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Helped by sympathetic Buddhists, he went through Gansu and Qinghai to Kumul and Turpan, then across regions that are today <b>Kyrgyzstan</b>, Uzbekistan and Afghanistan into Gandhara.</blockquote><a href="/work/journey-to-the-west">Journey to the West · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
